          %h2#Idea Idea
          %h3#basic_idea Basic idea
          %p
            Generally, a
            %em Fourier transform
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            between the algebra of
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            on a suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+group"} topological group
            and a
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convolution+product"} convolution product
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pontrjagin+dual"} Pontrjagin dual
            group. The study of Fourier transforms is also called
            = succeed "." do
              %em Fourier analysis
          %p
            Typically, such as in the case over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FourierTransformSmoothFunctionsWithRapidlyDecayingDerivativesOnCartesianSpace"}
            below) this means to decompose any suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superposition"} superposition
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+number"} complex
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane+waves"} plane waves
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/harmonic+analysis"} harmonic analysis
          %h3#generalizations Generalizations
          %p
            The concept of Fourier transforms of functions generalizes in a variety of ways. Core part of the subject of Fourier analysis is the generalization to
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier+transform+of+distributions"} Fourier transform of distributions
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FourierTransformOnTemperedDistributions"}
            below). The asymptotic growth of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier+transform+of+distributions"} Fourier transform of distributions
            reflects the singularity structure of the distributions, in dependence of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direction+of+a+vector"} direction
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+vector"} wave vector
            (the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+front+set"}> wave front set
            ”). The study of this behaviour is called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlocal+analysis"} microlocal analysis
          %p
            If the role of complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane+waves"} plane waves
            in the Fourier transform are replaced by
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                wavelets
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/wavelets"} ?
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %span.newWikiWord
                  wavelet transform
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/wavelet+transform"} ?
          %p
            For
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+topology"} noncommutative topological
            groups, instead of continuous characters one should consider
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreducible+representation"} irreducible
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+representations"} unitary representations
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+group+Fourier+transform"} quantum group Fourier transform
          %h2#general_definition General definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+compact+space"} locally compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+topological+space"} Hausdorff
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+group"} topological group
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haar+measure"} invariant (= Haar) measure
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo ,
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\in L_1(G,\mu)
            %strong Fourier transform
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{f}
            as a function on its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pontrjagin+dual"} Pontrjagin dual
            group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{G}
            given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi χ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi G
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
